  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<p>Correct answer: B</p>\n<p>A: For the first minimum, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\\sin\\theta=\\lambda\"></span>. Halving <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\"></span> increases <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\sin\\theta\"></span> (and hence <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\theta\"></span>), so it does not halve.</p>\n<p>B: For the first minimum, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\\sin\\theta=\\lambda\"></span>. If <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\\to a/2\"></span>, then <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\sin\\theta\\to 2\\lambda/a\"></span>, so for small angles <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\theta\"></span> approximately doubles.</p>\n<p>C: Since <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\\sin\\theta=\\lambda\"></span>, changing <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\"></span> changes <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\sin\\theta\"></span> and hence <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\theta\"></span>, so it cannot stay the same.</p>\n<p>D: Halving <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"a\"></span> doubles <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\sin\\theta\"></span> (and approximately doubles <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"\\theta\"></span> for small angles), not quadruples.</p>"
  },
